> But my main concern isn't so much bloat as it is clutter. Adding all those commits for tests would clutter up the log while the history of the tests and commits could be easily separated. I'm pretty sure unwanted tests commits will quickly show up in pull requests, which are complex enough to work with as it is.

Well, ideally changes to the code won't result in any changes to the test images, so there will be nothing to commit when you develop and re-run tests. This does agains raise the issue, though, that if you run the tests and nothing changes, it leaves no record in the git history, and so it's not possible to tell when the last time the tests were run -- you can only tell when the test results changed. Maybe there could be a dummy file in the visual_test/ directory that changes each time the tests are run; then you could find when the tests were last run, by asking git, "what was the most recent commit that changed this file?"

> Finally, it relies on the fact that the code of the original project is stored in a git repo, which decreases its versatility and generality.

